From tru64-unix-managers  Thu Jan 14 08:13:13 1999
From: Peter.Braack () degussa ! de
Date: Thu, 14 Jan 1999 08:13:13 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY:automounter trouble after vrestore
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=96079034715572

Dear admins,

my original question:

> on three of our servers (Alpha 4100, Digital Unix 4.0B Patchkit 8) th=
e
automounter hangs and the commands df and ls -l / are hanging to.
> I know that only a reboot stops the automounter in that state.

> Here is my question:
> The three troublesome servers all have in common that I enlarged / an=
d /usr
on saturday. All other servers of the same kind have no problems at all=
.
> The only problem I can think of is that I did the vdump/vrestore of /=
 and
/usr in multi user mode.
> Can you link the problems with automounter to that fact (open files..=
.)? How
to repair?

As usual - fast and valuable response from:

Bob Adams, adams@Kodak.com
Dr. Thomas P. Blinn, tpb@zk3.dec.com
Gerhard Nolte, gnolte@gwdg.de
Tom Leitner, tom@finwds01.tu-graz.ac.at
Dion Rivera, dion_rivera@hotmail.com

All of them pointing in the right direction - expecially Tom Leitner wh=
o
advised to remove /tmp_mnt after reboot. That's it!

For overall understanding let me quote the answer of Dr Thomas Blinn:
----------------------------------

If you can manage to kill off the automounter (or even if you can't, wh=
en you
shut down to single user mode), remove all the directories that are lef=
t over
from doing the dump of root and /usr in multiuser mode; when you do the=
 dump
in that way, you are going to pick up the automounter's directories as =
well as
its symlinks that point to where things are mounted; apparently these c=
onfuse
the automounter if you restore the root and /usr file systems from back=
ups in
that state.

I can't easily tell you what you need to clean up, but sometimes it's p=
retty
obvious.  It all depends on how much stuff gets automounted in your sit=
e and
where it gets mounted.

Tom
----------------------------------

Thanks you very much
Peter Braack
Peter.Braack@degussa.de
=

